We evaluate
$$L=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{n}{\ln n}\left(\frac{\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n}-\frac{1}{e}\right).$$

**Stirling expansion.** The Stirling series for $\ln(n!)$ is
$$\ln(n!)=n\ln n-n+\tfrac12\ln(2\pi n)+\tfrac{1}{12n}+O(n^{-3}).$$
Dividing by $n$,
$$\frac{\ln(n!)}{n}=\ln n-1+\underbrace{\frac{\ln(2\pi n)}{2n}+\frac{1}{12n^2}+O(n^{-4})}_{=:a_n}.$$
Note $a_n\to 0$ and $a_n=O(\ln n/n)$.

**Exponentiating.** Since $\sqrt[n]{n!}=\exp\!\big(\tfrac{1}{n}\ln(n!)\big)$,
$$\frac{\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n}=\frac{1}{e}\,e^{a_n}=\frac{1}{e}\left(1+a_n+\frac{a_n^2}{2}+\cdots\right).$$
Hence
$$\frac{\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n}-\frac{1}{e}=\frac{1}{e}\left(a_n+\frac{a_n^2}{2}+O(a_n^3)\right).$$

**Multiply by $n/\ln n$.**
$$L_n:=\frac{n}{\ln n}\left(\frac{\sqrt[n]{n!}}{n}-\frac{1}{e}\right)=\frac{1}{e}\left(\frac{n\,a_n}{\ln n}+\frac{n\,a_n^2}{2\ln n}+O\!\left(\frac{n\,a_n^3}{\ln n}\right)\right).$$

**Main term.**
$$\frac{n\,a_n}{\ln n}=\frac{n}{\ln n}\cdot\frac{\ln(2\pi n)}{2n}+\frac{n}{\ln n}\cdot\frac{1}{12n^2}+O\!\left(\frac{1}{n^3\ln n}\right)=\frac{\ln(2\pi n)}{2\ln n}+O\!\left(\frac{1}{n\ln n}\right).$$
Since $\ln(2\pi n)=\ln n+\ln(2\pi)$,
$$\frac{\ln(2\pi n)}{2\ln n}=\frac12+\frac{\ln(2\pi)}{2\ln n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\frac12.$$

**Remainder terms.** Using $a_n=O(\ln n/n)$:
$$\frac{n\,a_n^2}{2\ln n}=O\!\left(\frac{n}{\ln n}\cdot\frac{\ln^2 n}{n^2}\right)=O\!\left(\frac{\ln n}{n}\right)\to 0,$$
and similarly $\frac{n\,a_n^3}{\ln n}=O\!\left(\frac{\ln^2 n}{n^2}\right)\to 0$.

**Conclusion.** All terms except the main one vanish, so
$$L=\frac{1}{e}\cdot\frac12=\boxed{\frac{1}{2e}}.$$

